#{[17592186536823 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}]$"] [17592186536829 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[8(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$"] [17592186536833 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{12n+1}{3n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186536837 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[3(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$"] [17592186536861 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$"] [17592186537335 :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(x+h)-(2x+1)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186537339 :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{x-7(x+h)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186537349 :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(x+h)+(-7x)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186537468 :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{2(x+h)+1-(2x+1)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186537512 :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(x+h)+x}{h}\\right)"] [17592186537551 :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(x+h)+x}{h}\\right)"] [17592186537562 :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{2x+1-(x+h+1)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186537604 :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)-(-7x)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186537667 :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{2x+1-(x+h)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186537754 :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7x-(x+h)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186537884 :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=-7x$,  we have that $f(x+h)=-7(x+h).$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)-(-7x)}{h}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186537953 :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=2x+1$,  we have that $f(x+h)=2(x+h)+1.$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{2(x+h)+1-(2x+1)}{h}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186541054 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{t\\to -4}\\frac{{t}^{2}-16}{{t}^{2}+4t}.$"] [17592186541065 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 4}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-8}{{x}^{2}+x-20}.$"] [17592186541105 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{2x-6}.$"] [17592186541129 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x}{{x}^{2}-8x+15}.$"] [17592186545299 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2$ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=2$"] [17592186545316 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $"] [17592186545325 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2$ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $"] [17592186545334 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=0$"] [17592186545346 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2$ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $"] [17592186545384 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=0$ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $"] [17592186545439 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $"] [17592186545573 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=0$ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $"] [17592186545584 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=0$"] [17592186545604 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $"] [17592186545803 :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3d381842539a44e53d77697b18f0cb51552996c885a41e6681d56cba5025428.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$"] [17592186545803 :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=0.$ \n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $0$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty $ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186545808 :question/problem "![](images/b3/b35bd1000242bcbc00fcd5e1cd04d8f3b13e563f2df72234b438827949ad4ef5.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186545808 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as $x$ decreases to $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function moves closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches $2,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=2.\n$$"] [17592186545839 :question/problem "![](images/71/717e9728ec64332ffa0f115b8698fe5babac2b6c11e1764aeb28494b89a0c141.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\,$ and $\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}."] [17592186545839 :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=3.$ \n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $3$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function goes without bound to $-\\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186545855 :question/problem "![](images/07/07b3922c7b14ed6bb5c613f81485fc4e155232fe0958805b9437c7ca1b4e608a.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$"] [17592186545855 :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty .$\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty $ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$\nthe graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2.\n$$"] [17592186545871 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d6448b3b7899a447d4259d6e4d60ea33262306755485aafa7604e13b3709fca0.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$"] [17592186545871 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as $x$ decreases to $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function moves closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-2.$\n\nTherefore, as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches $-2,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-2.\n$$"] [17592186546967 :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{6{x}^{7}-3{x}^{3}-4}{2{x}^{5}+7}=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186547970 :question/problem "![](images/f4/f498fdcd8c6be58b8d1c1e6e4c3d835799a8c571cdd9125f7e7555307c94a6d1.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$"] [17592186547970 :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the limits in question.\n\nWe see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is true.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I and II are true."] [17592186548005 :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc6f40458a9a38615b9d1fe01c7e454611a56915d2dcaadaa8661c4d56defb6f.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$"] [17592186548005 :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the limits in question.\n\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ goes to $-\\infty ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function is not defined. So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."] [17592186548066 :question/problem "![](images/e3/e30b6d047b119e5beb5f0d5cf5e013628e7c9836e0ca264ee2da92d1e589a0fc.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186548066 :question/worked-solution "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$"] [17592186548071 :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cdb3e9c74a36e5ef13436b7b18968064b278f6d8e0dffc27820f618f7f4fee9.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186548071 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ goes to $-\\infty $, and therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186548979 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2),\\,\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$"] [17592186549000 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [17592186549007 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =\\pi ,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\pi $\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [17592186549008 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3$ for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [17592186549021 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [17592186549025 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=8{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta -8{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$"] [17592186550468 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186550497 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186550562 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186550616 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186550978 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{4i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186551235 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$"] [17592186551235 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{1}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=4{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=4{x}^{3}$ and $b=3.$"] [17592186551285 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/64/64b557365c3642ae7a4206794080804bf2bbccb07230dfa46a4f415570f85329.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}-5x=-5(-2)=10\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}(2)^{2}=10.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd37979eada411b68755dfa85ec3b1e22585df2bff5c4bdd4b27cf06463706d6.png)"] [17592186551347 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66b91f732d36469ded18df5404b3f12dddad7474168efd47ddf038835b67b831.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(-2x+1)=-2(-3)+1=7\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}({x}^{2}+1)=(3)^{2}+1=10.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement III is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/88/888053858d845bd47a56b953a5eb9a6f7f6b4ddffc0ecc70fa280e1a7d543f24.png)"] [17592186552420 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -5}\\frac{4x}{x+1}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186552439 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186552441 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186552441 :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-1.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $-1$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$  the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=-1.\n$$"] [17592186552447 :question/problem "Given that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}Cf(x)=8,$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186552464 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186552464 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$"] [17592186552495 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186552495 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=-1$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $1.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=-1$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $1.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=1.\n$$"] [17592186552511 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{1-{x}^{3}}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186552519 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186552519 :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186552523 :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea804b5eb5c4af6aace19a66008dfbf9b130f1e1e38c2d8d9d811e9cad4c413c.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)}{f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [17592186552523 :question/worked-solution "We find from the graph that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist, therefore\n \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)}{f(x)}=\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 0}({x}^{2}-1)}{\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)}=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186558095 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}+7{x}^{3}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186558137 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}-{x}^{3}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186558158 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(x+h)^{3}-7{x}^{3}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186558252 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{7(x+h)^{2}+7{x}^{2}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186558311 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{7(x+h)^{3}+7{x}^{3}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186562447 :question/problem "If $p(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ and $q(x)=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{6}},$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186562466 :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=q(x)=-3{x}^{3},$ what is $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{3}(x)\\cdot {q}^{2}(x))?$"] [17592186562478 :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=6{x}^{9}$ and $q(x)=15{x}^{7},$ what is $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)}{p(x)}?$"] [17592186562488 :question/problem "If $u(x)=14{x}^{11}$ and $v(x)=-7{x}^{11},$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}."] [17592186562499 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{8})=${{field-1}}"] [17592186562505 :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=5{x}^{7},$ what is $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))?$"] [17592186563647 :question/problem "Find $f(5)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(5)=⟨-1,1,-2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩\\,.\n$$"] [17592186563652 :question/problem "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(-7)=⟨6,0,-2⟩$ and $g(-7)=⟨2,-1,0⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)].\n$$"] [17592186563660 :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to -1}(\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186563661 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t),$ if $f(t)=⟨{e}^{t-2},\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,\\frac{3}{t+2}⟩.$"] [17592186563662 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\frac{4+t}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1}\\,j+\\sqrt{9t}\\,k.\n$$"] [17592186563676 :question/problem "![](images/94/94027c25ef78ae46d0deb4b577339daf878efd71b71029a9575f9c8ed9096e2c.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the function $f(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186563690 :question/problem "Find $f(0)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(0)=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\\,.\n$$"] [17592186563691 :question/problem "Find $f(0)$ given that $f(t)$ is a continuous vector-valued function and $u(t)$ is continuous real-valued function for which $u(0)=3$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$"] [17592186563714 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t),$ if $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+(t+2)^{2}\\,k.$"] [17592186563738 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)$, if $f(s)=⟨\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{s}^{3}-5⟩.$"]}
